# November 21, 2026: a second scatter across Alabama, still the state with the fewest places on the map (19).
# Ground claimed with tools/choose.py --thinnest (al). Spread from the Tennessee Valley (Florence, Decatur, Huntsville,
# Fort Payne) through Anniston, the Black Belt (Greensboro, Selma), Montgomery and the Wiregrass (Eufaula, Ozark) to
# Monroeville, Mount Vernon and Mobile. Nothing that tools/start.py listed was repeated.
# Sources read in this session: Wikipedia (tools/wiki_raw.py); the Alabama Historical Commission's visit and property
# pages; the museums' and churches' own pages (Weeden House, Monroe County Museums, Dexter Avenue King Memorial,
# St. Michael and All Angels); the Encyclopedia of Alabama (Forks of Cypress, Sturdivant Hall); the Muscle Shoals
# National Heritage Area (Forks of Cypress, Old State Bank); HMdb (Mount Vernon Arsenal marker, Claybank Church);
# exploresouthernhistory.com (Claybank Church). Read with tools/page.py.
from data_b import N
from data_ma import W
BATCH={'key':'al_zbty','label':'Alabama: a second scatter, Florence to Mobile','added':'2026-11-21 10:17'}

def AHC(page,label):
    return ['Alabama Historical Commission: '+label,'https://ahc.alabama.gov/'+page]
NOPARK=' Parking was not checked on the ground.'

PLACES=[
N('forks-of-cypress-florence','The Forks of Cypress (ruins)','Florence','al','landmark','ruins',34.84574,-87.72556,'exact','view','drive','burned','1830–1966',
  'A Greek Revival plantation house finished in 1830 for James Jackson and his wife Sally Moore Jackson, designed by William Nichols, on a slave-labour cotton and horse-breeding plantation where Big and Little Cypress creeks meet. It was the only house in Alabama with a two-storey colonnade all the way round, 24 Ionic columns. Lightning set it on fire in 1966 and it was never rebuilt; the Dowdy family, owners from 1936 to 1989, kept the ruin.',
  'The brick columns standing in a ring in the field: Wikipedia says all but one of the 24 were standing as of June 2017. The Encyclopedia of Alabama lists outbuildings, the Jackson family cemetery and an African American cemetery on the property. A near-replica of the house stands in downtown Florence. A 2010 photograph on Wikipedia shows bare red brick columns on a stone base.',
  'Two dozen columns standing round a house that burned in 1966.',
  'The sources disagree on ownership: Wikipedia and the Encyclopedia of Alabama say the ruin belongs to the State of Alabama (the Alabama Historical Commission), but the Commission\'s own page says the site is on private property and may not be accessible, and the Muscle Shoals National Heritage Area asks visitors to remember it is private property. The Encyclopedia says the access road is privately owned and the ruins are visible from the surrounding roads. Look from Jackson Road (the Heritage Area\'s directions: from Cox Creek Parkway north on Jackson Road, then right at the dead end); do not go up the private road.'+NOPARK,
  [W('The Forks of Cypress'),['Encyclopedia of Alabama: Forks of Cypress','https://encyclopediaofalabama.org/article/forks-of-cypress/'],
   ['Muscle Shoals National Heritage Area: Forks of Cypress','https://msnha.una.edu/sites-attractions/forks-of-cypress-plantation-site/'],
   AHC('properties/forksofcypress/forksofcypress.aspx','Forks of Cypress')],conf='mixed',brick='yes'),
N('old-state-bank-decatur','Old State Bank','Decatur','al','civic','standing',34.61431,-86.98365,'exact','view','walk','','1833',
  'The Decatur branch of the Alabama State Bank opened on July 29, 1833, one of three branches the legislature authorized in 1830; it lost its franchise over unpaid debts and stood empty. It was one of four buildings in Decatur to survive the Civil War, used by Union forces as headquarters and as a hospital. The First National Bank used it from 1881 to 1902, and it was restored in 1976 to 1983 and again in 1995 to 1996.',
  'A Jeffersonian bank with a five-column portico; Wikipedia says the limestone columns came from Trinity, Alabama. Inside not checked: the Muscle Shoals National Heritage Area says the bank is closed for restoration.',
  '',
  'Owned by the City of Decatur (Wikipedia links the Decatur Parks and Recreation Department). Closed for restoration, as the Muscle Shoals National Heritage Area page says; when it will reopen was not found. Seen from Bank Street.'+NOPARK,
  [W('Old State Bank (Decatur, Alabama)'),['Muscle Shoals National Heritage Area: Old State Bank, temporarily closed','https://msnha.una.edu/?p=2115']],brick='?'),
N('joseph-wheeler-plantation-pond-spring','Pond Spring, the Joseph Wheeler Plantation','Hillsboro','al','landmark','standing',34.64889,-87.25583,'area','open','walk','','1818–1880s',
  'John P. Hickman settled Pond Spring in 1818; Benjamin Sherrod bought it in 1827 and his son Felix enlarged the bigger of two log dogtrot cabins into the Sherrod House in the 1830s. Daniella Sherrod, widowed in 1861, married the Confederate cavalry general Joseph Wheeler, who lived here and later served in Congress and in the Spanish–American War. The Wheeler family gave the place to the state in 1993.',
  'Twelve historic structures from 1818 to the 1880s (Wikipedia), among them the log dogtrot cabin, the Sherrod House and the restored Wheeler House. The Commission\'s page offers guided tours and self-guided walks of the grounds; the gift shop is in the Sherrod House.',
  '',
  'An Alabama Historical Commission state historic site; admission charged (amount not checked). Hours and tours from the Commission\'s visit page; phone 256-637-8513.'+NOPARK,
  [W('Joseph Wheeler Plantation'),AHC('PondSpringPlanYourVisit.aspx','Pond Spring, plan your visit')],brick='?',
  hours='Wednesday to Saturday 9 a.m. to 4 p.m., closed noon to 1 p.m.; closed Thanksgiving, Christmas, New Year\'s Day'),
N('weeden-house-huntsville','Weeden House Museum','Huntsville','al','landmark','standing',34.72971,-86.58229,'exact','open','walk','','1819',
  'A two-storey Federal house built in 1819 for Henry C. Bradford; John McKinley, later a Justice of the US Supreme Court, was one of its owners. The Weeden family had it from 1845 to 1956, apart from the Civil War, when the Union Army took it over; the painter and poet Maria Howard Weeden lived most of her life here. It was cut into apartments after 1956, bought by the city in 1973, restored, and opened as a house museum in 1981.',
  'The 1819 house in the Twickenham district, shown by guided tour of about an hour (museum page). Interior fabric not checked.',
  '',
  'City-owned; run as a museum by the Twickenham Historic Preservation District Association. $10 adults, $5 children 12 and under, cash or PayPal only; tours begin at the times given and can be cancelled for private events (museum page).'+NOPARK,
  [W('Weeden House Museum'),['Weeden House Museum','https://weedenhousemuseum.com/']],brick='?',
  hours='Guided tours Monday, Wednesday, Friday 10 a.m. and 1 p.m.; Tuesday, Thursday 10 a.m.'),
N('fort-payne-depot-museum','Fort Payne Depot Museum','Fort Payne','al','rail','standing',34.44426,-85.71853,'exact','open','walk','stations','1891–1983',
  'The Alabama Great Southern Railroad built this depot in 1891, at the height of Fort Payne\'s mining boom, to a Richardsonian Romanesque design by Charles C. Taylor. Passenger trains stopped in 1970 and freight in 1983; it became a museum in 1986.',
  'Grey sandstone with pink granite trim, a hipped roof with gables and a round corner tower (Wikipedia). Inside: railroad memorabilia, dioramas made in 1913, Native American artifacts and local history, and a caboose (Wikipedia, Visit Lookout Mountain).',
  '',
  'A museum at 105 5th Street. Hours were not on any page read in this session: hours not checked.'+NOPARK,
  [W('Fort Payne Depot Museum'),['Visit Lookout Mountain: Fort Payne Depot Museum','https://visitlookoutmountain.com/?p=4047']],brick='no',
  hours='?'),
N('st-michael-all-angels-anniston','St. Michael and All Angels Episcopal Church','Anniston','al','worship','standing',33.66683,-85.84361,'exact','view','walk','','1888–1890',
  'Given to the city by John Ward Noble in memory of his father and brother, designed by William Halsey Wood of Newark; ground was broken on June 11, 1888 and the church was consecrated on September 29, 1890. Its tower follows the Norman churches of the Nobles\' native Cornwall. Sandstone came from nearby Rocky Hollow on a narrow-gauge railway built for the job, and two English masons shaped every stone, including the wall round the grounds.',
  'The parish\'s history page describes an interior of oak and longleaf pine with carved angel heads on the hammer beams, a twelve-foot white Carrara marble altar and an alabaster reredos with archangels, oak pews and screens, an Alabama marble chancel floor, windows by J. and R. Lamb of New York and two Tiffany windows. Interior seen only in the parish\'s description; visiting times not checked.',
  '',
  'An active Episcopal parish at West 18th Street and Cobb Avenue. Service times were not found on the pages read; see the parish site.'+NOPARK,
  [W('St. Michael and All Angels Episcopal Church (Anniston, Alabama)'),['St. Michael and All Angels: history','https://stmichaelsanniston.org/?p=130']],brick='no'),
N('magnolia-grove-greensboro','Magnolia Grove','Greensboro','al','landmark','standing',32.70414,-87.608,'exact','open','walk','','1840',
  'A Greek Revival house built in 1840 for Isaac Croom, a lawyer and planter, and his wife Sarah Pearson Croom, named for the 15-acre grove of magnolias round it. It passed to the Hobson family; their son Richmond Pearson Hobson, a naval hero of the Spanish–American War, received the Medal of Honor in 1933. The Alabama Historical Commission runs it as a house museum.',
  'The house with original family furniture and portraits of the Croom and Hobson families, a kitchen with cook\'s quarters, a slave house and exhibits on the 12-acre site (Commission page). Guided tour of the house with admission.',
  '',
  'Alabama Historical Commission state historic site on Hobson Street; admission charged (amount not checked); first floor and grounds accessible. Phone 334-624-8618.'+NOPARK,
  [W('Magnolia Grove (Greensboro, Alabama)'),AHC('MagnoliaGrovePlanYourVisit.aspx','Magnolia Grove, plan your visit')],brick='?',
  hours='Friday and Saturday 10 a.m. to 4 p.m.; other days by appointment; closed state holidays'),
N('sturdivant-hall-selma','Sturdivant Hall','Selma','al','landmark','standing',32.41297,-87.0289,'exact','open','walk','','1852–1856',
  'A Greek Revival mansion begun in 1852 and finished in 1856 to Thomas Helm Lee\'s design for Colonel Edward T. Watts. The banker John McGee Parkman bought it in 1864 and was killed in 1867 trying to escape from the jail at Cahaba; the merchant Emile Gillman\'s family owned it until 1957, when the City of Selma bought it. Edward Vason Jones called it one of the finest Greek Revival houses in the Southeast.',
  'A two-storey brick house stuccoed to look like cut stone, with a cupola, and a kitchen, smokehouse and two-storey servants\' quarters forming a courtyard behind (Wikipedia). Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Owned by the City of Selma and kept by the city and the Sturdivant Museum Association, at 713 Mabry Street; admission charged. The museum\'s own site did not answer: hours are from the Encyclopedia of Alabama, not checked with the museum.'+NOPARK,
  [W('Sturdivant Hall'),['Encyclopedia of Alabama: Sturdivant Hall Museum','https://encyclopediaofalabama.org/article/sturdivant-hall-museum/']],brick='no',
  hours='Tuesday to Saturday 10 a.m. to 4 p.m. (Encyclopedia of Alabama; not checked with the museum)'),
N('edmund-pettus-bridge-selma','Edmund Pettus Bridge','Selma','al','landmark','standing',32.40556,-87.01861,'site','public','walk','','1940',
  'A steel through-arch bridge of 1940 carrying US 80 Business over the Alabama River, named for a Confederate general, senator and Klan leader. On March 7, 1965, Bloody Sunday, police attacked civil rights marchers here with clubs, horses and tear gas as they set out for Montgomery; they crossed again on March 21 and walked to the Capitol. It became a National Historic Landmark in 2013.',
  'The 250-foot central steel arch with ten concrete spans, nine of them arches on the east side; the bridge tender\'s house still stands at the bridge (Wikipedia).',
  'Where Bloody Sunday happened, and the marchers crossed again two weeks later.',
  'A working four-lane highway bridge with sidewalks; a public street. Mind the traffic.'+NOPARK,
  [W('Edmund Pettus Bridge')],brick='no'),
N('dexter-avenue-king-memorial-church','Dexter Avenue King Memorial Baptist Church','Montgomery','al','worship','standing',32.37728,-86.3027,'exact','open','walk','','1883–1889',
  'Organized in 1877 by freedmen and free people of color as the Second Colored Baptist Church; the brick church was begun in 1883 and finished in 1889. Martin Luther King Jr. was its pastor and helped organize the Montgomery bus boycott from here in 1955. It became a National Historic Landmark in 1974 and took its present name in 1978.',
  'The 1889 red brick church (Wikipedia photograph) steps from the State Capitol, with the Legacy Center and a statue of King behind it; the parsonage is a museum (Wikipedia). Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'An active church. Church tours by appointment only; no tours Sundays or Mondays; phone (334) 261-3270 (church tours page). The parsonage museum keeps set hours.'+NOPARK,
  [W('Dexter Avenue Baptist Church'),['Dexter Avenue King Memorial: tours','https://dexterkingmemorial.org/tours/']],brick='yes',
  hours='Church tours by appointment; parsonage museum Friday and Saturday 10 a.m. to 4 p.m.'),
N('fendall-hall-eufaula','Fendall Hall','Eufaula','al','landmark','standing',31.89157,-85.15715,'exact','open','walk','','1856–1860',
  'An Italianate villa of wood frame with a cupola, built between 1856 and 1860 by Edward Brown Young, a New York-born banker, and his wife Ann Fendall Beall. Young is credited with changing the town\'s name from Irwinton to its Muscogee name, Eufaula. Five generations of the family lived here; the Alabama Historical Commission bought it in 1973 and restored it to how it looked from 1880 to 1916.',
  'The house restored to 1880–1916; first floor accessible, with a lift to the porch (Commission page). Interior fabric not checked.',
  '',
  'Alabama Historical Commission state historic site on West Barbour Street; admission charged (amount not checked). Phone 334-687-8469.'+NOPARK,
  [W('Fendall Hall'),AHC('FendallHallPlanYourVisit.aspx','Fendall Hall, plan your visit')],brick='no',
  hours='Monday to Saturday 10 a.m. to 4 p.m.; closed state holidays'),
N('claybank-log-church-ozark','Claybank Log Church','Ozark','al','worship','standing',31.44701,-85.66223,'site','public','walk','cemeteries','1852',
  'A log church built in 1852 by a Methodist minister, the second on the site after one of 1829–1830, and probably the oldest building in Dale County. The congregation moved to a new church nearer town in 1873 and the log church was used only now and then after 1900. The Claybank Memorial Association bought it in the 1960s and restored the interior in 1980.',
  'One room about 30 by 25 feet, walls of pine logs 12 to 15 inches thick dovetailed at the corners (Wikipedia), with shuttered windows, under pines beside a kept cemetery. The interior is restored to its 19th-century look (exploresouthernhistory.com).',
  '',
  'Owned by the Claybank Memorial Association since the 1960s (Wikipedia); on on Andrews Avenue (Highway 289) near US 231. Open daily in daylight, no charge, as exploresouthernhistory.com says; the association\'s own page was not found.'+NOPARK,
  [W('Claybank Log Church'),['Explore Southern History: Claybank Church','https://exploresouthernhistory.com/claybankchurch.html']],brick='no',
  hours='Daily in daylight; free (exploresouthernhistory.com)'),
N('old-monroe-county-courthouse','Old Monroe County Courthouse','Monroeville','al','civic','standing',31.52703,-87.32429,'exact','open','walk','','1903–1963',
  'The county courthouse from 1903 to 1963, in the town where Harper Lee and Truman Capote grew up. Capote wrote of it, and it was the model for the courthouse in To Kill a Mockingbird: the film\'s designers measured and photographed the courtroom and built a copy on a Hollywood sound stage. It was renovated from 1991 to 2002 and became a National Historic Landmark in 2021.',
  'The courtroom restored to its 1930s look, where visitors may walk the balcony, the witness chair and the judge\'s bench; exhibits on Lee and Capote; plays of To Kill a Mockingbird are staged here (museum page, Wikipedia). A Wikipedia photograph shows red brick walls under a white cupola with clocks.',
  'The courtroom Hollywood copied for To Kill a Mockingbird.',
  'Run by the Monroe County Museums; admission $5 a person; closed most holidays; phone 251-575-7433 (museum page).'+NOPARK,
  [W('Old Monroe County Courthouse'),['Monroe County Museums: Old Courthouse Museum','https://monroecountymuseum.org/old-courthouse-museum']],brick='yes',
  hours='Monday to Friday 10 a.m. to 4 p.m., Saturday 10 a.m. to 2 p.m.'),
N('church-street-graveyard-mobile','Church Street Graveyard','Mobile','al','burial','standing',30.68583,-88.05083,'site','public','walk','cemeteries','1819–1898',
  'Founded in 1819 to replace the Campo Santo, where the cathedral now stands, as Mobile\'s main burial ground; the city acquired it in 1820. It was split into a Catholic third, a Protestant third and a western "graveyard for strangers", where Masons, Odd Fellows, veterans and the poor were buried. It closed to burials in 1898.',
  'Four acres inside a brick wall of 1830, with early 19th-century carved stones from New England and the Gulf Coast and early wrought and cast iron (Wikipedia, City of Mobile page). A Wikipedia photograph shows red brick tombs and plot walls among the stones.',
  '',
  'Owned by the City of Mobile (Wikipedia; listed among the city\'s cemeteries on its parks page); a city park and cemetery downtown. Hours were not given on the city page read: hours not checked.'+NOPARK,
  [W('Church Street Graveyard'),['City of Mobile: Church Street Graveyard','https://www.cityofmobile.gov/parks-rec/cemeteries/church-street-graveyard/']],brick='some'),
N('mount-vernon-arsenal','Mount Vernon Arsenal and Searcy Hospital','Mount Vernon','al','fort','standing',31.08812,-88.02418,'area','view','drive','burned','1828–2012',
  'The US Army set up this arsenal in 1828 near the Mobile River; with the Kennebec Arsenal in Maine it is one of the most complete antebellum arsenals left. Alabama militia seized it in January 1861. As Mount Vernon Barracks it held Geronimo and other Apache prisoners from 1887 to 1894; it went to the state in 1895 and from 1910 was a hospital for Black psychiatric patients, renamed Searcy Hospital in 1919, which closed in 2012. A fire on April 10, 2026 collapsed the roof of the arsenal tower building (Wikipedia, citing Fox10).',
  'Many of the 1828 arsenal buildings remain (Alabama Historical Commission, 2019), behind the hospital grounds. Named to the National Trust\'s 11 Most Endangered list in 2019 and the Commission\'s Places in Peril for 2025. What the 2026 fire left was not checked.',
  'Geronimo\'s prison, a segregated asylum, and now an empty arsenal that burned in 2026.',
  'Closed state property (the former Searcy Hospital); look only from the public road. The pin is the historical marker at East Coy Smith Highway (County Road 96) and Superintendents Drive (HMdb). Do not enter the grounds.'+NOPARK,
  [W('Mount Vernon Arsenal'),['HMdb: Mt. Vernon Arsenal and Barracks / Searcy Hospital','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=70593'],
   ['Alabama Historical Commission: 11 Most Endangered, 2019','https://ahc.alabama.gov/news_detail.aspx?ID=13387']],brick='?'),
]

